Skip memory-backend-shm where the C library has no POSIX shared memory.

bionic has neither shm_open nor shm_unlink — and could not usefully have them,
because Android has no /dev/shm for them to open files in. QEMU 9.1 added
backends/hostmem-shm.c unconditionally for every non-Windows host, so an
Android build stops there with "call to undeclared function 'shm_open'".

The object only registers the `memory-backend-shm` QOM type, which exists for
sharing guest RAM with a vhost-user process. Nothing we run has one: the VM's
only backend is the default anonymous mapping. Dropping the file costs the
build nothing and is preferable to shimming shm_open onto a directory Android
does not have.

Probed through the compiler rather than switched on host_os, because QEMU
reports an NDK target as plain 'linux' and would take this branch anyway.

--- a/backends/meson.build
+++ b/backends/meson.build
@@ -13,7 +13,9 @@
 if host_os != 'windows'
   system_ss.add(files('rng-random.c'))
   system_ss.add(files('hostmem-file.c'))
-  system_ss.add([files('hostmem-shm.c'), rt])
+  if cc.has_function('shm_open')
+    system_ss.add([files('hostmem-shm.c'), rt])
+  endif
 endif
 if host_os == 'linux'
   system_ss.add(files('hostmem-memfd.c'))
